{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact head 546b3e39a5d17eb7acee01ea9ee68fc2a3a3d6b8 (branch request/commitment-loop-cut-current-main; single commit on current main 316d5aeb). The cut itself is sound and I verified its central claims; two small in-scope items block approval.\n\nARCHITECTURE: affected layer is the Workroom application profile (governed kinds, deterministic fold, commitment lifecycle, fold-profile version). Its contract CHANGES and the change is declared in the same head: docs/reference/architecture.md updates the commitment-lifecycle and merge-succession bullets, and internal/workroom/schema.go bumps ProfileVersion workroom-fold@3 -\u003e @4. That bump is correctly wired: internal/app/checkpoint.go stamps Profile from Workspace.foldProfile(), so a fold@3 checkpoint cannot be reused under a fold@4 binary. I checked the new init-time application binding landed in main: this repository carries no gitseq/app-binding@0 record in refs/seq, so selectHost returns the default host and the bump forces a cold rebuild rather than the \"verifiable but uninterpretable\" refusal at internal/app/host.go:101. Kernel, gitstore, intent are untouched. The cmd/gs merge gate is untouched: closure is purely projective and rides on the existing validateMergeReceiptNow. internal/statusview and the UI consume Commitment.Report only as an opaque chain member (query.go:336,340; ThreadPane.tsx:185,218), so an artifact id in that field breaks no consumer. Contract change is declared, not silent.\n\nSECURITY: no authority boundary is weakened, and the ratification claim is enforced rather than asserted. Artifact-as-report is tightly bound in latestCompletion: RenderArtifact, non-empty commit, record author == promise author, and exactly one promise basis which must be that promise. Closure requires mergedArtifacts, which requires a live sealed receipt whose mergePlan is non-nil, and validateMergeReceiptNow yields a plan only when the approval is effective, non-retired, RATIFIED, verdict approved, head == merge_candidate, the artifact is cited by the approval and stands at that candidate, artifact author != approval author (independence), and receipt author == artifact author. So an implementer cannot self-close: artifacts carry SatisfierNone and cannot be ratified at all, and the only other route to satisfied is an explicit report ratified by the originating requester (decideRatify, \"only the requester may declare satisfaction\"). The board stays legible: promised -\u003e reported (waiting_on = requester) -\u003e satisfied. The approval bases are written by the reviewer, not the implementer, and every admitted artifact must stand at the reviewed candidate commit, so a merge cannot close a commitment for unreviewed work. What genuinely changes is that the original requester no longer signs a post-hoc acceptance; that judgement now rests on the request conditions plus an independent ratified approval. That is exactly what request 3b3f3437 authorized and AGENTS.md/architecture.md now say so. Non-mergeable resolution still needs an explicit report or supersession, and is tested.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: the cut is clean. No half-removed ceremony: zero stale \"ready-for-review\" instructions remain anywhere in the tree, the how-to examples were rebased from --rests-on $REQUEST to $PROMISE with $PROMISE actually defined in both scripts, and the removed steps left no dangling $REPORT / $MERGED_ARTIFACT / $MERGE_COMMIT references. All 30 durable citations in the changed pages resolve to effective records.\n\nGATES at the exact head, run by me in a clean worktree at 546b3e39: go build ./... OK; go vet ./... OK; go test ./... -count=1 EXIT=0, all 21 packages ok including internal/docset (458.8s) and internal/workroom (3.2s). Mutation check of the new seams, run in a separate worktree so the gate was undisturbed: dropping the promisor-authorship condition, widening len(promiseBases)==1 to \u003e=1, and dropping !f.ratified(approvalID) were each killed by the new tests (TestOnlyThePromisorsSinglePromiseArtifactActsAsAReport twice, TestMergeDoesNotCloseCommitmentWithoutExplicitApprovalRatification).\n\nTWO ITEMS TO FIX:\n\n1. docs/README.md:42 still summarises the changed page as \"Request, promise, report, ratification - and who is allowed to close what.\" That is the twin of the summary this head rewrote in docs/concepts/work-loop.md front matter (\"How promises become exact artifacts, independently approved merges, or explicit reports\"), and it now names report-and-ratification as the spine of the loop while omitting artifacts and merges entirely - the ceremony this head removes. I considered calling it trivial and did not: it is the docs index entry for the very page whose contract changed, it is the first description a reader meets, and it sits under the docs candidate artifact path so it needs no new artifact. Update it to match the page.\n\n2. The merger-identity guard in mergedArtifacts, \"artifact.record.Actor != receipt.record.Actor\", has no test. I removed it and the whole suite still passed, so nothing protects it from regression. The guard is correct and load-bearing: without it a merge signed by one implementer could close a different actor s commitment whenever the reviewer cited both artifacts at the same candidate head, which is precisely the cross-author widening validateMergeReceiptNow s own comment says the receipt must not mint. Please add the negative test alongside the seven you already have.\n\nEverything else above I verified at this exact head and it needs no rework; re-review should be quick once these two land.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d4dca0689b8564a26318b42cdd3fe5fe825f0866","head":"546b3e39a5d17eb7acee01ea9ee68fc2a3a3d6b8","verdict":"changes-requested"}}